  <section id="how-to-read">
    <h2>How to read this page</h2>
    <p>
      This is the most detailed Arabic account of Samkough so far. It is also
      someone else’s published work, so I have <strong>not</strong> translated
      it in full. This page summarizes it in my own words and translates four
      short sentences. Please have an Arabic reader check the translations, in
      particular the names of schools and tribes.
    </p>
    <p>
      <mark>Highlighted text marked “Review”</mark> is something I’m unsure of
      or that disagrees with another source on this site. All of the
      biographical details are attributed to Qabartay, and the article gives
      no references for them.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="summary">
    <h2>Summary</h2>
    <p>
      The article calls him <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">أمين أيوب سمكوغ</span>
      (“Amin Ayyub Samkoug”, with his father’s name, Ayyub, in the middle) and
      says he was among the most important Syrian Circassians, known for wisdom
      and good judgment. In Qabartay’s account:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Birth and school.</strong> Born in 1900 in the village of
        al-Mansura in Quneitra governorate. Primary school in Quneitra, then
        the Anbar school (<span lang="ar" dir="rtl">مكتب عنبر</span>) in
        Damascus on a free Ottoman government scholarship, because he was the
        son of a “martyr”: his father died in Tripoli, Libya, defending it
        against the Italian invasion. He then got his secondary certificate.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>France.</strong> He learned French in Beirut for a year, and
        went to the Sorbonne, where he earned a diploma in general agriculture
        and another in international politics.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Paris society.</strong> He was secretary of the Circassian
        society in Paris, in touch with Circassian communities across Europe
        (Syrian, Lebanese, Turkish and from the Caucasus) and worked to
        strengthen cultural ties. He studied antiquities, Circassian history and
        manuscripts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Parliament.</strong> After returning to Syria he won a seat in
        the Syrian parliament in 1930, representing Quneitra.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Society and school.</strong> He founded the Circassian Literary
        Society on 4 March 1933, and the mixed (boys and girls) Adyghe-Circassian
        school in Quneitra, which ran from 1933 to 1943. He meant to expand it
        into a preparatory school teaching trades, especially agriculture. On
        17 May 1938 he set up branches of the society in Circassian villages,
        including Reyhaniye near Antioch and the Quneitra villages of Adnaniya
        and Khushniya.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The 1937 company.</strong> He founded the Golan Trading Company
        in Quneitra with local capital, to encourage Circassians into
        self-employment, in farming, trade and the professions, under
        “Republican Decree no. 262”.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Newspaper.</strong> He helped found and run the first Circassian
        newspaper in the diaspora, <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">«مارج»</span>
        (<em>Marj</em>), published in Quneitra in Arabic, Circassian, French and
        Turkish. Later he became its editor-in-chief.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Mayor.</strong> He led the Quneitra municipality, improved it,
        attached some neighbouring villages to its services, and was “the first
        independent president” in Quneitra.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Peacemaking.</strong> He ended tribal disputes between the
        recently settled Circassians and the Druze, the Bahatra and the Na’im,
        among others.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Languages and works.</strong> He read and wrote Arabic,
        Circassian, French and Turkish, and sang Circassian poems and music in
        his spare time. His works: “The Circassian Alphabet in Latin Letters”,
        “Circassian Poems” and “History of the Circassians from Ancient Times to
        the Modern Era”. A manuscript of “Excerpts from Historians’ Accounts of
        the Circassians’ Heroism in Their Wars against the Tsars” was lost
        before printing. His son, Barzaj (Berzeg) Samkoug, published
        “Introduction to the History of the Circassians”, which is the
        introduction to the “History”.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Death.</strong> May 1952, buried in the cemetery of the Qabartay
        quarter of Quneitra.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
