  <section id="review">
    <h2>Things to review</h2>

    <h3>Conflicts with other sources on this site</h3>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>when he was mayor.</strong> The article says he was
          the <em>first</em> head of the Quneitra municipality in 1937. Mirzoev
          has him as chairman from 1928 to 1936 and removed after the clash of
          9 September 1936. Both cannot be right, and the 1937 date comes right
          after the year in which, in Mirzoev’s account, he lost the post. The
          article also calls him “the first independent president” without
          saying what “independent” means.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>parliament in 1930.</strong> Mirzoev says 1931.
          Kushkhabiev has the end of 1931, and one Arabic article gives March
          1933. This is a fourth date.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the society’s date and name.</strong> Here it is the
          “Circassian Literary Society”, founded on 4 March 1933. Mirzoev has
          the “Society for the Promotion of Education and Culture” in 1927, with
          a Literary Union formed inside it. They may be different bodies, or the
          1933 date may be a later re-founding.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>branches in May 1938.</strong> The article says he
          opened society branches on 17 May 1938. In Mirzoev’s account the
          society had been banned after September 1936. The Turkish profile
          says the branches were school branches, not society branches.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>his father.</strong> Here he <em>died</em> in Tripoli
          (the son got a scholarship as a “martyr’s” son). The Turkish profile
          says only that he fought there in 1911.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>studies.</strong> Here, one year of French in Beirut
          and then the Sorbonne, with diplomas in agriculture and in
          international politics. The Turkish profile has two years in Beirut
          and the schools of Grignon and Sciences Politiques. Mirzoev has an
          agronomist qualification in Beirut and the Sorbonne. The Sorbonne
          would be an unusual place for an agriculture diploma, so one of these
          names is probably loose.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the newspaper.</strong> Here he helped found it, and
          “later” became editor-in-chief. Mirzoev gives the editor-in-chief as
          Hagur Tarik Mumtaz and gives no date for any change. No years are
          given here at all. The Marj issue I transcribed is in Ottoman Turkish
          with an Arabic box.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>his books.</strong> Here the book on the wars against
          the Tsars is a manuscript that was <em>lost before printing</em>, in
          1967. The Turkish profile says it was published in Homs in 1948, and
          Mirzoev says Amman, 1984. Here the <em>Introduction</em> was published
          by his son, with no place or year. Mirzoev gives Damascus, 1984, and
          the Turkish profile says only “the 1980s”.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>death.</strong> May 1952. Mirzoev and the dictionary
          entry say 1953, and the biography page says 1952, with no month.
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Unverified details</h3>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>“Republican Decree no. 262”.</strong> A decree number
          and a Syrian “republican” decree in 1937 under the French Mandate are
          plausible, but I couldn’t check either.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>“Anbar school”.</strong> I read
          <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">مكتب عنبر</span> as the well-known Anbar
          school in Damascus (an Ottoman-era secondary school). The article
          doesn’t explain it.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>tribe names.</strong> “Bahatra” and “Na’im” are my
          reading of <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">البحاترة</span> and
          <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">النعيم</span>, which I take to be Arab
          tribes of the Golan area. No dates or events are given for the
          peacemaking.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the Paris society.</strong> It is not named, and it
          is not said to be the Mountaineers’ People’s Party, which the other
          Turkish pages and the dictionary entry put him in. This article never
          mentions that party.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the grave.</strong> The city of Quneitra was largely
          destroyed in 1967 and has been in the buffer zone since, so I can’t
          tell whether the cemetery or the grave still exists.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>tone.</strong> Phrases like “wisdom and good
          judgment” are praise, not fact. The article gives no sources for
          anything, other than naming Qabartay.
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Where it agrees with other sources</h3>
    <p>
      Much of it overlaps with the Turkish profile
      (<a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/bilgiler-emin-semsug/">2009</a>,
      <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/circassian-center-emin-semsug/">2019</a>):
      his father’s Libya link, the school branches in Reyhaniye, Adnaniya and
      Khushniya, the 1937 company, the four-language newspaper, the peacemaking
      and the works, including the Latin-letter alphabet, which matches
      Mirzoev. So the two probably share a source. It is not a confirmation.
      One new point is useful: the 1937 “Golan Trading Company” has the same
      name as Mirzoev’s “Jolan”, which supports the idea that they are one
      body, though the purposes described still differ.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="translation-notes">
    <h2>Translation notes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The four quoted passages are short, but Arabic news prose and names can
        be read in more than one way. An Arabic reader should check them.
      </li>
      <li>
        I have spelled names as the article does: “Samkoug”, “Qabartay”, “Barzaj”.
        “Qabartay” is also the name of the quarter where he is said to be
        buried, and the same word as “Kabardian”.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Read the full article in Arabic at
      <a href={sourceUrl}>esyria.sy</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
